    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Visible blood in the urine with difficult urination (R31.0)</content>. CT urography shows a 4.2 cm sessile mass of the posterior bladder wall extending to the dome, with wall thickening and perivesical stranding, clear upper tracts, and no enlarged nodes; the chest CT is clear. Cystoscopy today found a solid, partly necrotic tumor with white keratinizing plaques. Urine culture showed no growth, and cytology showed atypical squamous cells suspicious for malignancy. A muscle-invasive bladder cancer is likely, possibly of squamous type; resection will give the diagnosis and depth. Hemoglobin 11.2 g/dL is stable. [S]<list><item>Transurethral resection of the bladder tumor with examination under anesthesia in two days, as Dr. Bergström-Nwosu advises.</item><item>Catheter stays in until after the resection.</item><item>Call for clots, a blocked catheter, or fever.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute kidney injury from bladder outlet obstruction (N17.9)</content>. Creatinine 1.24 mg/dL (eGFR 58), down from 1.68, BUN 22 mg/dL, and potassium 4.6 mmol/L after a week of drainage: resolved, close to his creatinine of 1.1 a year ago. [S]<list><item>Kidney function with each blood test during treatment.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Benign prostatic hyperplasia with lower urinary tract symptoms (N40.1)</content>. Prostate about 78 mL on CT with a median lobe; enlarged, firm, smooth gland on examination; PSA 6.4 ng/mL, a PSA density of 0.08, in keeping with the gland&apos;s size and the retention, catheter, and bleeding. By shared decision, no prostate MRI or biopsy: at 83, with a bladder tumor that will set his course, a prostate cancer found now would not change treatment. [S]<list><item>Continue tamsulosin.</item><item>Trial without catheter after the resection, when he is free of infection.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic urinary retention (R33.8)</content>. Drained by catheter; the anticholinergic sleep aid has been stopped. [S]<list><item>Catheter care reviewed; leg bag by day and a night bag.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic constipation (K59.09)</content>. Bowels regular on polyethylene glycol alone after stopping the phosphate enemas, which carry a risk of dangerous phosphate levels with reduced kidney function. [S]<list><item>Continue polyethylene glycol daily.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Upper-tract imaging: multiphasic CT urography, with chest CT</td><td>Once, in the initial evaluation of gross hematuria</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Done two days after intake and reviewed today: bladder mass with possible extravesical extension, clear upper tracts, no nodes; chest clear</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cystoscopy</td><td>Once, in the initial evaluation of gross hematuria</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Flexible cystoscopy by urology today: 4 cm solid sessile tumor of the posterior wall and dome</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine culture and urine cytology</td><td>Once, in the initial evaluation of gross hematuria (cytology for gross hematuria by expert consensus)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Results from the first visit reviewed today: culture no growth; cytology atypical squamous cells suspicious for malignancy</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Kidney function and potassium after relief of obstruction</td><td>Within one to two weeks of catheter drainage</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Creatinine 1.24 mg/dL (eGFR 58), BUN 22 mg/dL, potassium 4.6 mmol/L; recovered</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin during gross hematuria</td><td>At each visit while bleeding continues</td><td>Completed today</td><td>11.2 g/dL, stable from 11.6</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PSA interpretation</td><td>Once, when it would change management</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6.4 ng/mL with a prostate of about 78 mL (density 0.08); no MRI or biopsy by shared decision</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>IPSS</td><td>Baseline and at every follow-up visit (CMID BPH)</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not scored while the catheter drains the bladder; 22 at intake before the catheter; next after the trial without catheter</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Post-void residual</td><td>At baseline when retention is suspected, after a trial without catheter, and when symptoms change</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Catheter in place; 460 mL at intake; repeat after the trial without catheter</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Digital rectal examination</td><td>Baseline, then as clinically indicated</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at intake: enlarged, firm, smooth, no discrete nodule</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Alpha-blocker tolerability (orthostatic blood pressure, dizziness)</td><td>At 4 to 12 weeks after starting, and at each visit in a frail older adult</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No postural drop (132/70 to 128/68 mmHg); no dizziness</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review for drugs that worsen retention or kidney function</td><td>At intake and at each change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Diphenhydramine and sodium phosphate enemas stopped; no NSAIDs; no other anticholinergics</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Catheter care</td><td>While a catheter is in place</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Leg and night bags, hand hygiene, bag below the bladder, fluids 1.5 to 2 liters a day</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Urology consultation</td><td>For gross hematuria and for retention with kidney injury</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; resection booked in two days</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find the cause of the bleeding within three weeks. In progress; target 1778-04-22 [S]</item><item>Relieve the retention and recover kidney function. In progress; target 1778-04-22 [S]</item><item>Treatment that controls bleeding and local symptoms while keeping him at home and writing. In progress; target 1778-05-27 [S]</item><item>Pain at or below 3 of 10 without sedation, falls, or constipation. In progress; target 1778-05-27 [S]</item><item>A written plan for future care, with a named health care agent. In progress; target 1778-04-28 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Microhematuria: AUA/SUFU Guideline (2020; amended 2025) (2025); Treatment of Non-Metastatic Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer: AUA/ASCO/SUO Guideline (2017; Amended 2020, 2024) (2024); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Bladder Cancer, Version 3.2026 (including non-urothelial histologies) (2026); EAU Guidelines on Muscle-invasive and Metastatic Bladder Cancer (2026); Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); Diagnosis, Prevention, and Treatment of Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection in Adults: 2009 International Clinical Practice Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2009); American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults (2023); Prostate Cancer: Screening (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, urologic cancer and benign prostatic hyperplasia monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Resection in two days; chronic-care review with the pathology in about ten days; sooner for clots, a blocked catheter, or fever. Next scheduled visit 1778-04-22: review of resection pathology, diagnosis, geriatric assessment, trial without catheter, and shared decision on treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

